<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;Isn&rsquo;t the Trinity a logical contradiction? 3 = 1?&raquo;</h4>
<p>It is not a contradiction because we are not saying that three Persons are one Person, or that three gods are one God. We say that three Persons share one divine nature. It is like three persons who share the same humanity: they are not the same person, but they are the same nature. The difference is that in God, the nature is one in a perfect and absolute way, not analogically as in us (CCC 252-255).</p>

<h4>&laquo;Why are Christians monotheists if they believe in three gods?&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is a common question, especially in dialogue with Jews and Muslims. The answer is clear: Christians believe in one God. The three Persons are not three gods, but three Persons in one God. The Catechism states it forcefully: &laquo;We do not confess three gods, but one God in three Persons&raquo; (CCC 253). The Persons are distinct by their relations, not by their nature.</p>

<h4>&laquo;Aren&rsquo;t the analogies (clover, water, sun) heresies?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Every analogy of the Trinity is limited and, if taken too far, can fall into heresy. The clover (three leaves, one plant) approaches modalism (one Person with three forms). Water (solid, liquid, gas) is the heresy of modalism. The sun (disc, light, heat) comes closer, but is still limited. The important thing is to use analogies as clues, not as complete explanations. The Trinitarian mystery surpasses every human analogy (CCC 253-255).</p>

<h4>&laquo;If the three Persons act together, why do we say the Father creates, the Son redeems, and the Spirit sanctifies?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The external works of God (opera ad extra) are common to the three Persons. The Father creates, but does so through the Son and in the Holy Spirit. However, tradition attributes certain works to each Person in a way appropriate to them: creation to the Father (as origin), redemption to the Son (as incarnate), sanctification to the Holy Spirit (as gift). It is not that they act separately, but that each Person acts according to their proper relation within the Trinity (CCC 257-260).</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Glory to the Father, and to the Son, and to the Holy Spirit,<br />
God one and triune,<br />
who has called me to speak of your greatest mystery.<br />
Give me simple words for an immense mystery,<br />
a heart that burns with your Trinitarian love,<br />
and the humility to know that I do not explain the Trinity:<br />
I adore it, I love it, and I live it.<br />
May those who hear me not remain in ideas,<br />
but encounter You,<br />
communion of love that invites us to enter.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

